The hierarchical model keeps the per-region parameters and gives them a shared parent:

$\log y_i \sim \mathcal{N}(\beta_{r[i]}, \sigma), \qquad \beta_j \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu, \tau), \qquad \mu \sim \mathcal{N}(5, 1), \quad \tau \sim \text{half-}\mathcal{N}(0.5).$

```r
fit_partial <- brm(
  log(ms) ~ 1 + (1 | region),
  data   = latency,
  family = gaussian(),
  prior  = c(prior(normal(5, 1), class = Intercept),
             prior(normal(0, 0.5), class = sd),
             prior(normal(0, 1), class = sigma)),
  chains = 4, iter = 2000, seed = 20260803,
  control = list(adapt_delta = 0.95)
)
```

`adapt_delta` is raised because the funnel geometry between $\tau$ and the $\beta_j$
produces divergent transitions at the default of $0.8$ when the group count is small
[@stan2024guide]. At $0.95$ the run is clean.

**Convergence.** $\hat{R} \le 1.01$ on every parameter including $\tau$, zero
divergences, and tail ESS above 900 for $\tau$ — the lowest of any parameter in the
report, which is the expected signature of a group-level scale estimated from five
groups.

**Shrinkage, measured.** The posterior mean for `ap-south` moves $0.11$ on the log scale
toward the global mean relative to its no-pooling estimate, while `us-east` moves less
than $0.01$. The pull is proportional to how little each region says on its own, which is
the behaviour @fig-model-hierarchical encodes.

**Sensitivity.** Refitting with $\tau \sim \text{half-}\mathcal{N}(1)$ and
$\tau \sim \text{half-}\mathcal{N}(0.25)$ moves every region's posterior mean by less
than $0.03$ on the log scale. The group-level prior is weakly identified, as noted, but
it is not driving the conclusion.
